# KNOWN GAP — a RATIFIED safety rule is not enforced at two of its three
# positions. `AGENTS.md` (Ownership at Consuming Positions) states that for the
# single-owner carve-outs -- `Box[T]`, `Callable[T]`, `Owned[T]`, `Task`,
# `TaskGroup`, `Guard` -- the safety pass emits `E_MoveWithoutOperator`
#
#     "at bare-assign sites AND at constructor / struct / enum-init sites,
#      forcing the user to write `^source` or `source.clone()`"
#
# Measured 2026-08-27, `gg check` only:
#
#     Box[String] b = a                       LOCAL source   -> REJECTED  ✅
#     Box[Box[String]] o = Box[...](a)         LOCAL source   -> ACCEPTED  ⛔
#     Box[String] taken = h.b                  FIELD source   -> ACCEPTED  ⛔
#     Box[Box[String]] o = Box[...](h.b)       FIELD source   -> ACCEPTED  ⛔
#
# So enforcement exists ONLY at bare-assign-from-a-local. The CONSTRUCTOR
# position is unenforced entirely, and a FIELD source escapes at both positions.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND STRICTNESS: the accepted programs are exactly the
# ones that then double-free at runtime. The ratified rule, enforced, would
# REJECT them at check time -- which is the reference-grade outcome (Core #10,
# lower-or-reject) rather than lowering something the language says is illegal
# and hoping the drop model copes.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): `gg check` REJECTS with E_MoveWithoutOperator,
# directing the user to `^h.b` or `h.b.clone()`.
#
# Found 2026-08-27 during R45 Track A brief-review pass 4; orchestrator-verified.

struct H:
    Box[String] b

void main():
    H h = H(Box[String]("x"))
    Box[Box[String]] outer = Box[Box[String]](h.b)
    print("built")
